feat: derive, queue and cancel activity occurrences

Tasks 5, 6, 7 and 10. The scheduling half, proved against real Postgres.

`activity_occurrences(from, to, as_of)` reduces both schedule shapes to a
set of instants and writes nothing. Keeping it separate from the queueing
function is the most useful decision here: it means a test can ask what a
schedule produces on a given day in a given zone without anything being
queued, claimed or delivered.

The `as_of` parameter exists for exactly one reason — the daylight-saving
case. Without it the choices are mocking Postgres's clock or
reimplementing the schedule arithmetic in the test, and the second only
proves two implementations agree with each other. With it, the test asks
the real function what a 09:00 London schedule produces either side of
the 2026-03-29 transition and asserts one occurrence each day, 09:00 on
the wall clock both times, and 09:00 UTC before against 08:00 UTC after.
That last pair is what proves a conversion happened rather than a
wall-clock string being stored and handed back.

Three local days are generated per schedule, not one. A window the user
thinks of as "today" can straddle `from_at` in UTC, and 00:15 local is a
different UTC date from the same user's 23:45. Generating yesterday,
today and tomorrow and filtering by the requested range is the simple way
to be right at every offset.

`queue_due_activities()` runs every 15 minutes with a five-minute
look-back and a twenty-minute lookahead, so consecutive runs see the same
occurrence deliberately. That is only safe because
notifications_unique_per_occurrence makes the insert idempotent — the
guarantee is in the schema, so rewriting this function cannot lose it. A
test calls it four times and asserts the row count does not grow.

Cancellation mirrors sync_fast_notifications: when the thing a
notification describes changes, the pending queue is wrong and is
cancelled, not corrected. Requeueing is left to the next run rather than
done in the trigger, because recomputing occurrences there would put the
schedule arithmetic in a second place. Triggers are scoped to the columns
that move the time, so renaming a custom activity does not drop a
reminder due in the next quarter hour. A time-zone change cancels that
user's pending rows, which is what stops someone who flies to London
getting yesterday's queue at the wrong local time.

One thing the tests caught immediately: the queue function returned 11
where the test expected 1, because `resetUserData` did not know about the
new tables and rows accumulated between cases. Cleanup now has its own
ordered list, separate from USER_TABLES, because it must include
activity_schedules — which cannot be in the generic RLS suite at all,
since a schedule needs a parent activity and there is no minimal
standalone row for it.

So activity_schedules gets its own RLS cases, and they cover the one
thing no other table needs: it has a denormalised user_id that RLS trusts
and a trigger owns. A test inserts a forged user_id and asserts the
trigger overwrote it, then that the row is invisible to the user it was
forged onto. Without that trigger a client could file a schedule under a
stranger's id, invisible to its real owner, queueing notifications
attributed to someone else.

189 database tests pass.
